            <ArticleTitle>Testosterone and type 2 diabetes in men.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-24</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/13685538.2013.879113</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess from observational data if low testosterone in men is an independent risk factor for high fasting glucose (FG) and for a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes (T2D).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Multivariate analysis of data from 991 male US Air Force veterans who completed six medical examinations over 20 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Low testosterone was moderately related to high FG, independent of age and obesity. Low testosterone is a very weak predictor of a diagnosis of T2D.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In men, low testosterone is an independent risk factor for high FG, comparable to aging and obesity. Low testosterone is a weak predictor of a diagnosis of T2D.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
